<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ethiopian highlands</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ethiopian highlands are a large mountainous plateau in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>. Their fertile land and natural defences made them the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire</a> and its Christian successors, and their invasion by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a> in the sixteenth century came close to destroying the Ethiopian state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ethiopian-highlands.jpg" alt="Ethiopian highlands" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hulivili · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Semien_Mountains_13.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The economy was therefore structurally dependent on control of the routes connecting the <b>Ethiopian highlands</b> to the coast: conflict with Ethiopia was not merely ideological but commercially necessary, since Ethiopian control of the highland markets would divert trade away from Adalite intermediaries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Aksumite Empire stands as one of Africa's most consequential ancient civilisations: its adoption of Christianity in the <b>Ethiopian highlands</b>, its development of the Ge'ez script and its integration of north-east Africa into the commercial networks of the ancient world created a cultural and institutional legacy that has…</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The eleven rock-hewn churches of Lalibela, cut downward out of the basalt of the <b>Ethiopian Highlands</b>, are traditionally ascribed to the Zagwe king Gebre Meskel Lalibela (r. c. 1181–1221), who is said to have meant them as a second Jerusalem, though their dating is disputed.</blockquote><a href="/work/rock-hewn-churches-of-lalibela">Rock-hewn churches of Lalibela · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>It lay in the south-eastern <b>Ethiopian highlands</b>, probably near Bale.</blockquote><a href="/state/dara-sultanate">Dara Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>He hoped to conquer the <b>Ethiopian highlands</b>, and the province's seat moved between Massawa, Suakin and later Jeddah.</blockquote><a href="/state/habesh-eyalet">Habesh Eyalet</a></li></ul></section>
